From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kreijstal <rainb@tfwno.gf>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:55:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Search the partial index while a scan is running

So far nothing could be found until a scan had completely finished, which
can take hours. Now, when there is no complete index to search yet (first
scan, or no database), the scan publishes sorted snapshots of what it has
found so far and searches use those:

- The index store takes a snapshot on the scanning thread from its progress
  event: right away, then at a growing interval (at least ten times the time
  the last snapshot took to build, at most every two minutes), and once more
  with all entries before the final sort orders are built.
- A snapshot is a store sorted by name only, without indices of its own; it
  keeps the indices its entries belong to alive. If a scan whose partial
  result was handed out fails, its entries stay alive with the index instead
  of being discarded.
- The database only searches a snapshot while it has no complete index: an
  existing (if outdated) index is still searched until the new one is done.
  A snapshot is never saved, and if its scan doesn't finish the previous
  store comes back.
- The new "scan-snapshot" signal makes windows search again and the status
  bar show the number of entries found so far.
